@@ -0,0 +1,72 @@
+# T006/T007 identity lock protocol — current-source amendment
+
+Status: **PROPOSED / SELF-EVALUATED / NOT ACCEPTED FOR IMPLEMENTATION.** Author Codex, 12 October 2026. Claude is the intended independent reviewer. This amends [the historical proposal](T006-identity-lock-protocol-design.md); its original body and candidate rejections remain intact. The prior owner-required design challenge ended without a verdict. No production code, schema, migration or gate changes accompany this amendment.
+
+Scope and acceptance were recorded before execution in [the continuation task](T006-identity-design-20261012.md). Baseline main `2992a78cc77112569740abf0522d399994c77910`. R04/R13/R15/R16, D01/D13, I05/I06/I07/I10/I11/I13; AC01/AC02 partial, AC40/AC73–AC75; M1 identity. User, OAuthAttempt, Session, XAccount, WalletIdentity, WalletChallenge, Participant, WalletLink and ModerationEvent are the concrete records. No money, destination, claim, work-capacity or provider effect is proposed here.
+
+## Findings resolved in this proposal
+
+| ID   | Old proposal or current fact                                                                                                                          | Required amendment                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |
+| ---- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
+| LP01 | W7 was described as a resolver that skips primary wallets; the current lookup checks WalletLink first.                                                | A linked result locks its owner and never enters folding. A null result holds the subject advisory key but no User row, so the primary fold may still acquire its X keys before roots. Remove the unconditional §8 claim that released lookup necessarily folds while holding L2. Keep a test proving this control-flow boundary.                                        |
+| LP02 | W8 takes balance before challenge consumption; L0 was declared globally first.                                                                        | Keep released balance-before-claim order. Define the single challenge claim as a leaf exception with one challenge per transaction and no later challenge acquisition. Prove all challenge writers, including the sweep, satisfy that boundary; do not silently reorder financial exclusion.                                                                             |
+| LP03 | The proposed keep/callback roots use NO KEY UPDATE even when xId changes.                                                                             | User.xId has an ordinary unique index. PostgreSQL upgrades those updates to FOR UPDATE. Take the final required root strength before any identity child lock; the initial implementation proposal below uses FOR UPDATE for identity writer roots. The real database primitive experiment observes an xId UPDATE waiting behind KEY SHARE after NO KEY UPDATE succeeded. |
+| LP04 | Proposed L3 order said XAccount before Session, while W9 actually reads Session before XAccount; merge prelocks sessions then modifies X/wallet rows. | Use one concrete child acquisition order, prelock all existing child resources that may be used before mutation, and forbid lower-order acquisition after mutation starts. Nested merge receives an already acquired plan and may not discover another account/key or upgrade a lock.                                                                                    |
+| LP05 | W3 selected `owner.xId OR profile.id`, not the complete affected X-key set; unlocked owner assumptions were presented as stable.                      | Plan the complete existing account/X-key set, acquire all keys in one order, acquire all roots, then re-read and compare. Changed membership or identity aborts the whole transaction; never append a newly discovered lower key/root. Keep atomic first-X binding as a defense even after roots.                                                                        |
+| LP06 | A materialized SKIP LOCKED sweep was described as never waiting, and startAttempt could retain sweep locks before taking advisory/root locks.         | Only tuple waits in these cleanup tables are skipped. DDL/table locks can still wait. Run cleanup as its own autocommit statement with no later work in that transaction. Check actual schema for delete-side FKs/triggers. Preserve rows skipped under contention and retry next schedule.                                                                              |
+| LP07 | W7/W8 implicit FK locks and synthetic identity writers were incompletely inventoried.                                                                 | Session creation takes a User FK lock even on primary sign-in. Include every app writer and isolate/adapt sandbox fixture writers; standalone Participant provisioning is an external root-lock consumer. Audit unique/trigger/FK acquisition, not only explicit SELECTs.                                                                                                |
+| LP08 | String ordering and advisory classes were treated as an unconditional deadlock proof despite hash-derived aliases and implicit locks.                 | Record concrete SQL lock identities, namespace collision assumptions and nonprotocol host consumers. Require reviewer disposition on collision handling and interface compatibility; make no general deadlock-free claim from this matrix or six primitive checks.                                                                                                       |
+
+## Current writer inventory
+
+This is an inspected source inventory, not a statement that the protocol is already implemented. The source manifest records the exact files.
+
+| Writer                                          | Current order / boundary                                                                                                                                                             | Proposed conformance                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |
+| ----------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| X start                                         | Unlocked Session/User read; optional own X advisory; global expired-attempt DELETE; attempt INSERT. OAuthAttempt has no User FK.                                                     | Separate skip-locked expiry statement before entering the start transaction. Snapshot own session/account, acquire X key and account root, lock own Session, read database time, revalidate, insert attempt. No orphan authenticated attempt if the account disappeared. Anonymous start takes no account root.                                                                                 |
+| X claim / callback finally                      | One PK UPDATE / DELETE, each autocommit. Claim remains durable during provider exchange; no DB transaction spans the provider call.                                                  | Keep these leaf statements. Verify cancellation and expiry again in completion. No child lock survives into another transaction.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |
+| X completion                                    | One X advisory; delete own claimed attempt; unlocked session/owner reads; optional nested merge; User bind/upsert, XAccount upsert, Session delete/create.                           | Determine profile owner and initiating account; all needed X keys before sorted roots; validate complete ownership/session/attempt state under locks. Prelock children before delete/upsert. Fold direction chosen only after roots/emptiness checks; nested execution acquires nothing new. Atomic User binding remains. New X account creation is serialized by profile key and unique index. |
+| X disconnect                                    | Unlocked User read; own X advisory; unordered attempts DELETE; XAccount DELETE.                                                                                                      | X key, own root, revalidate xId, ordered children, delete only own attempts/XAccount. Permanent User.xId and unrelated accounts preserved.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |
+| Merge helper                                    | Dropped X advisory; ordered drop attempts; sorted roots NO KEY UPDATE; emptiness; wallet/X movement; unordered Session DELETE; dropped User DELETE upgrades lock.                    | Complete precomputed identity plan; all X keys before strong sorted roots, then owned children. Check mapping/activity/suspension and revalidate primary ownership before moves. Strong drop/keep locks taken up front, no upgrade at DELETE/xId update. Existing reject/no-partial-effect semantics preserved. Direct helper callers must use the same plan entry point.                       |
+| Wallet verification                             | One challenge claim; subject advisory; linked-only root/link lookup; primary reads; optional fold; own Session DELETE RETURNING; Session INSERT/FK. Whole transaction bounded retry. | Retain link-result/no-fold boundary, released flag-independent linked sign-in and same-account reauth. Plan primary/current roots and any fold X keys before root locks. Revalidate primary/reservation owner after roots. Claim remains in the same transaction; own-session authority and provenance cannot transfer from a foreign/expired token.                                            |
+| Wallet rotation                                 | Balance advisory; challenge claim; X/primary reads; new-subject lookup; primary delete/create; audit. Trigger takes new-subject key.                                                 | Preserve balance before claim. Explicit new-subject key before own root; revalidate X/primary/pending-transfer/cooldown under root, then ordered children and primary replacement. Trigger key is already held. No payout policy/cooldown change.                                                                                                                                               |
+| Additional link                                 | Challenge claim; subject; User NO KEY UPDATE; Session SHARE then XAccount SHARE; database clock; Participant KEY SHARE/create; WalletLink INSERT/subject trigger. Disabled/unwired.  | Subject then strong own root; prelock children in agreed order; authority clock only after grants. Consume proof/insert mapping/link atomically. Module needs an explicit compatible host prelocked-root contract; no hidden new direct DB singleton in the public port.                                                                                                                        |
+| Credential resolver/revoke module               | Subject, own root NO KEY UPDATE, credential/mapping reads or update. Revoke has no deployed host/session/audit composition.                                                          | Same subject/root prefix; host owns attribution, invalidation, durable audit and atomicity. Contract/example tests must preserve standalone use; changing root strength alone is not a verified host composition.                                                                                                                                                                               |
+| Admin suspension                                | Global 729103; User UPDATE; unordered own-account Session DELETE; audit. Approval no longer used.                                                                                    | Global advisory prefix, own root before identity children; ordered matching sessions; suspension and invalidation/audit atomic. All-session suspension policy remains distinct from credential-specific revoke.                                                                                                                                                                                 |
+| Logout                                          | One own Session PK DELETE, autocommit.                                                                                                                                               | Leaf deletion; may wait on an in-flight authority read but takes no later lock.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |
+| Worker expiry                                   | Separate autocommit global Session, WalletChallenge and OAuthAttempt deletes, currently unordered/waiting.                                                                           | One separately committed skip-locked statement per table. Preserve `< now` worker and `<= now` start semantics, existing scheduler reporting and rate-limit cleanup. No new retention policy.                                                                                                                                                                                                   |
+| Sandbox / fixtures                              | Root upserts, XAccount/WalletIdentity upserts, Session creation across separate statements; financial balance lock for test funds.                                                   | Dedicated synthetic target only; adapt writers that can run concurrently with identity commands to the same plan, or document and enforce exclusive fixture setup. Fake X/test-USDC remain unmistakable.                                                                                                                                                                                        |
+| Participant provisioning / nonidentity FK users | Provisioning takes User KEY SHARE, then Participant INSERT; finance/campaign paths have balance/global prefixes and implicit User KEY SHARE from inserts.                            | Include these edges in challenge tests. Root holders cannot later request balance/global keys. No new financial table write from identity. A merge's strong root must precede emptiness and protect against a new FK-dependent insertion.                                                                                                                                                       |
+
+## Proposed acquisition protocol for challenge
+
+1. **Plan from unlocked reads.** Gather the exact self/profile/primary/reservation account set and all required X/subject/balance keys. Record observations for comparison. No client role or owner field is accepted as an observation. New account IDs are server-generated; uniqueness failure rolls back.
+2. **Advisory prefix.** Existing host global/financial keys precede subject and X keys. Within each class use one documented deterministic order. Acquire every key before roots; triggers must reacquire an already held subject key. The legacy 64-bit hash namespace can alias classes: independent review must either accept a qualified collision assumption for this bounded protocol or require a compatible namespace/migration design. No unconditional graph proof is asserted.
+3. **Account roots.** Lock deduplicated IDs one at a time in an explicitly fixed order shared by every writer, initially FOR UPDATE for identity writer roots. Do not use a database collation order in one caller and JavaScript string order in another. Reject absent/changed roots or ownership after locks. Strong roots intentionally wait for pre-existing FK KEY SHARE holders and prevent new FK activity during a dropping merge. Cost/compatibility must be reviewed; weakening roots later needs evidence against every implicit upgrade.
+4. **Owned children.** Prelock existing rows across all named roots in one table order: OAuthAttempt → XAccount → Session → WalletIdentity → Participant → WalletLink; within each table, fixed primary-key order. Use a separate lock statement for each sorted PK when cross-collation agreement is uncertain. Use the final needed row strength before mutation. Reads for link authority may use SHARE; rows to delete/update use UPDATE. Preserve post-lock database-clock expiry validation.
+5. **Revalidate and mutate.** Re-read own live session, suspension, permanent X identity, profile ownership, reservation/primary owner, current proof/clock and merge activity. Any changed planned set aborts without adding a lock. Execute only the chosen plan; nested helpers take no previously unheld prefix/root/child locks. Inserted rows may require uniqueness/FK locks: list them explicitly and test competitors, including rootless legacy/fixture writers. Keep durable audit in the same transaction. Existing bounded whole-callback retry/sanitized errors stay; retry is not the proof of ordering.
+6. **Leaf exceptions.** Wallet commands claim exactly one challenge once; rotation may take its balance lock first. No writer holding other identity resources subsequently requests a challenge held by a different identity command; prove this inventory obligation. Single-PK autocommit claim/finally/logout and separately committed skip-locked cleanup hold no lock into subsequent work. A failing obligation blocks protocol acceptance.
+
+An acquired plan should be an internal typed host object, created by a shared helper, consumed by nested merge. It records account IDs, strongest modes and observations; it is not a client DTO or an authorization grant. Public reusable identity ports need a documented prelocked mode or compatible root upgrade that never occurs after child acquisition, contract tests and a consuming example. Choose that interface at design review before changing existing standalone contracts.
+
+## Primitive observations and limits
+
+The author experiments use the same actual User unique index and all repository migrations in a fresh PostgreSQL 16 target. `database-primitives.test.ts` observed: xId UPDATE blocked by KEY SHARE despite prior NO KEY UPDATE; non-key suspension update completed while KEY SHARE remained held; materialized cleanup skipped a locked expired attempt, removed only an unlocked expired row, retained a live row and removed the skipped row after release; analogous Session/challenge deletion progressed with the holder open; separate sorted root statements left the higher row lockable NOWAIT while the lower waited. These six checks prove their concrete statements only. They are not GREEN application fixes or a global protocol proof.
+
+PostgreSQL's [row-lock documentation](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/16/explicit-locking.html#LOCKING-ROWS) describes key-column UPDATE strength; its [SELECT locking documentation](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/16/sql-select.html#SQL-FOR-UPDATE-SHARE) limits SKIP LOCKED to row locks. Cleanup tables currently have no delete-side child FK or trigger work, but a future migration must recheck that premise. DDL/maintenance and privileged manual writes are outside a live application row-lock proof; they require operational coordination.
+
+The three original attempt deadlocks reproduce on current source with actual blocker PIDs and unchanged no-deadlock/rollback assertions. Additional links were false, folds true only in this dedicated process; production folds and linking remain off. The direct internal merge helper is callable regardless of the gate; these experiments do not claim that deployed routes bypass the fold gate. Current-source sanitized results and raw local evidence paths are in the continuation packet.
+
+## B2c/T007 dependencies
+
+The owner chose credential-specific session invalidation, canonical DEC-2026-10-12-05. Proposed follow-up schema: nullable server-populated additional credential ID on Session, an index for invalidation, a same-account FK or equivalent enforceable attribution fence, and no client-supplied ID. Exact DDL is not accepted by this design packet. An X-authenticated or primary-wallet session has no additional credential ID. A wallet-authenticated replacement must preserve actual source provenance, including same-account reauth while presenting another credential; tests must prevent attribution laundering. Revoke ACTIVE→REVOKED, delete matching sessions and write audit in one transaction under the same root protocol; sign-in arriving after revoke denies, sign-in winning first leaves a matching session that revoke removes.
+
+Existing null-attribution sessions cannot be assigned a credential from `via=wallet` alone. Do not invent backfill. Before enablement decide an authenticated rollout treatment (for example a reviewed expiry/drain window or explicit reauthentication) and verify it on every instance. Suspended self-revoke remains OPEN. The owner resolved visible signed destination binding as DEC-2026-10-12-06: account ID and connected X handle must appear in the additional-link message. Prepare a server-derived signed snapshot and exact account/intent/digest checks; do not accept a client handle or rewrite legacy intents. Define canonical serialization, handle-change compatibility and pending target-less challenge treatment in the implementation proposal. A retained current-source failing probe checks both visible fields; it is RED evidence only, not an application fix. AC02 nonce/domain/intent/network tests remain mandatory; the configured SIWS network fix and real Phantom retest status are separate evidence. Recovery, capacity, UAT, G10 and full T006/T007 acceptance stay open.
+
+## Claude independent challenge and implementation exit
+
+Claude should review this source as read-only, use their own disposable database, and supply a design verdict **ACCEPT FOR IMPLEMENTATION / ACCEPT WITH AMENDMENTS / REJECT**, including counter-schedules. No agent has been launched or messaged. Challenge LP01–LP08, the complete writer/implicit-lock inventory, ordering exceptions, root strength and FK cost, hash/collation assumptions, prelocked module contract, and selective-revoke provenance/rollout. Distinguish source inspection from executed evidence and retain every NOT RUN item.
+
+After acceptance, implement a single coherent test-first protocol slice. Required regressions: three retained deadlocks in both meaningful queue orders; same paths with link flag off; fold-gated denial preserving state; primary/link sign-in versus rotation/revoke/merge; first-X both winners; X disconnect and authority expiry while waiting; admin invalidation and worker cleanup versus session creation/merge; standalone provisioning/FK insertion versus drop/key update; lower-root/child NOWAIT ordering; post-lock ownership changes; changed membership abort; bounded retry with unchanged semantic identity. Fault injections must remove each ordering/skip/revalidation/strong-root mechanism and be killed. A skipped/no-match mutation is no evidence.
+
+Preserve account activity/mapping protection, all original migration bytes, released linked sign-in independent of link-creation flag, atomic own-session authority, integer/network money invariants, sanitized errors and default-off gates. Then execute focused database/contracts/consuming-host checks, full relevant regression/static/planning checks, a production build and timed SELF-EVALUATED record. Browser preview is required when the slice adds visible UI/routes; this design-only slice has no changed browser behavior. Claude's design acceptance will not supply independent implementation/UAT/release acceptance.
