+# Author response to Claude — R1–R8
+
+Codex, 12 October 2026. **DESIGN RECONCILIATION / SELF-EVALUATED; revised design independently NOT VERIFIED.** This response does not authorize implementation or release. The [revised design](design-reconciliation.md) controls the next proposed protocol slice; conflicting portions of the [previous amendment](../../../docs/tasks/T006-identity-lock-protocol-20261012.md) remain historical evidence.
+
+Reviewed [Claude's original report](../T006-identity-design-20261012-claude-review/REPORT.md), [probe script](../T006-identity-design-20261012-claude-review/review-probes.mjs), [results](../T006-identity-design-20261012-claude-review/probe-results.json), and [source identity](../T006-identity-design-20261012-claude-review/source-identity.json). All 15 recorded source hashes match this working tree; main HEAD is `2992a78cc77112569740abf0522d399994c77910`. No probe was rerun and neither disposable database was touched. Execution evidence below belongs to Claude's PostgreSQL 16.15 environment, not a new author run.
+
+| Amendment                                    | Decision   | Technical response and evidence                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        | Required next evidence                                                                                                                                                                                                      |
+| -------------------------------------------- | ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| R1 — plan account lock strength              | **MODIFY** | Accept rejection of uniform `FOR UPDATE` and final strength before acquisition. X1/X2 demonstrate new FK wait edges; X3 confirms `xId` needs strong protection. Delete/key-changing roots use `FOR UPDATE`; non-key updates and account-wide identity serialization use `FOR NO KEY UPDATE`. Modify ordinary authorization-sensitive sign-in to `FOR SHARE`: `FOR KEY SHARE` permits suspension's non-key update, so it alone cannot stabilize the `suspended` authorization predicate. `KEY SHARE` is sufficient only for an existence/key-only dependency with no non-key authority decision. Existing X completion assigns `xId` even on reconnect: it cannot be called weak until its SQL write footprint avoids the key column. See design §§2–3. | Claude challenge the SHARE/status race, exact ORM SQL, both X1/X2 queue orders, and the uniform-strong-lock mutation. Weak modes alone do not fix strong-root merges against legacy writers.                                |
+| R2 — coherent writer conversion and fence    | **ACCEPT** | X1 is a delete-child/insert-FK cycle; converting only merge roots leaves it intact. Convert all six named model writers, `User`, challenge composition, participant provisioning, admin and sandbox host consumers together. Include indirect FK consumers in the inventory, not just explicit identity SQL. AST/type-based architecture enforcement with narrow leaf exemptions and negative fixtures is specified in §5. Current `studio-sandbox.ts` autocommit writes are not a conforming production host.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         | Exhaustive call-site/SQL/FK inventory, CI fence bypass mutations, fixture isolation, all-instance version/drain evidence. An image containing both web and worker code is not an atomic deployment.                         |
+| R3 — merge wallet subject keys               | **ACCEPT** | Choose explicit subject locking, not a roots-only alternative. X4 records zero advisory locks on `WalletIdentity.userId` movement; the trigger fires on insert/address update, not reassignment. Plan old/new/moved addresses on both accounts, lock before roots, re-read ownership under roots and abort/re-plan any changed set. Include balance exclusions for both merge accounts before subjects. §3 forbids a nested merge from acquiring omitted keys.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         | Resolver-versus-merge in both queue orders, including primary reassignment, rotation, revoke and a mutation omitting moved subjects. No additional-link movement is authorized.                                             |
+| R4 — key builder, hash and ordering          | **MODIFY** | Accept one shared builder, byte-identical trigger contract, code-unit ordering and one parameterized lock statement per ID. Retain class order global → balance → subject → x-auth and existing labels/seed/key space. Modify the probability claim: `2^-64` is a pairwise idealized collision estimate, not a measured guarantee or a global bound; `hashtextextended` is not a cryptographic namespace proof. Detect different labels aliasing within a plan and fixed-key registry, deduplicate identical resources, fail closed on detected aliasing. Cross-plan undetected aliases remain an explicit assumption and independent risk, §3.                                                                                                        | Application/trigger key equality, fixed bigint keys, code-unit versus locale order, multiple keys per class, collision/fault tests. No unconditional deadlock-free claim.                                                   |
+| R5 — batched leaf cleanup                    | **ACCEPT** | Materialized ordered `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` selection plus `DELETE USING`, positive bounded LIMIT and independent autocommit batch. Preserve worker `< now` and start `<= now`. Do not hold cleanup tuple locks into start's identity transaction. Recheck delete-side FK/trigger effects on each migration. Skipping tuple waits does not skip table/DDL locks. §4 bounds both batch size and work per invocation.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  | Sweep-versus-new merge, retained locked rows, backlog/fairness/load, schema-change guard and exact expiry semantics. Primitive passing checks are not production backlog evidence.                                          |
+| R6 — LP01/02/04/05/07 and re-planning        | **ACCEPT** | Retain LP01 linked-result/no-fold boundary. Retain balance-before-one-challenge-claim for rotation, with immutable purpose/prefix rules and no later challenge. Use final child strengths/order before mutation; acquire the complete account/key set before guarded revalidation. Changed membership is a typed internal re-plan, not immediate denial for benign two-tab races. Whole-transaction rollback, fresh unlocked planning and bounded retry preserve nonce/session/audit semantics; genuine revoked/foreign/expired authority remains denial, §6. LP07 incorporates X2 and all implicit FK consumers.                                                                                                                                      | Same-digest race, cancellation during OAuth retry, changed plan/strength, exactly-one committed session/audit and no repeat provider exchange, budget exhaustion.                                                           |
+| R7 — already-locked module mode              | **ACCEPT** | Keep standalone public ports working without hidden app state or a host plan. Add an explicit versioned lock capability bound to the same live transaction, covered accounts/subjects, final strengths, child set and phase. It authorizes skipping acquisition only; it is never proof/authentication. Missing/stale/foreign/inadequate capability fails before mutation; no partial fallback or upgrade. Standalone commands use the shared planner themselves with today's safe `NO KEY UPDATE` account serialization. Hook restrictions, invariants and two consuming examples are in §7.                                                                                                                                                          | Real-DB standalone and prelocked contract suites, negative capability tests, alternate port, authority-after-wait tests and independently runnable examples under R15/R16. A TypeScript brand alone cannot prove SQL locks. |
+| R8 — extra schedules and mechanism mutations | **ACCEPT** | Add X1 and X2 in both queue orders to retained author deadlocks; assert state, authority and side-effect counts as well as completion. Add same-wallet sign-in/rotation/revoke/merge and new sweep/merge schedules. Every ordering, skip, revalidation and final-strength protection has a specific killed mutation, §9.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               | Independent deterministic backend-observed tests with no hidden application retry masking `40P01`; mutation failures must demonstrate the targeted defect. Real finance/campaign interleavings and load remain separate.    |
+
+No amendment is rejected. R1/R4 modifications require Claude's explicit design disposition. Acceptance of an amendment is the author's design decision, not independent acceptance of the revised design or any T006/T007 criterion.
+
+Additional findings: accept composite-FK credential attribution and a no-backfill **pre-enable check**, subject to all-instance coverage, bounded TTL/drain and zero-link evidence in every target environment. Accept SIWS **Resources binding** with exact stored account consent, a preserved handle snapshot and current connected-X authority; real Phantom Resources rendering and the outstanding staging/devnet invalid-format/network retest remain **NOT VERIFIED**. Details and boundaries are in design §8.
